**Proposed Derivations and Fixes**\
\
**Explicit line-by-line derivation of \\(\\frac\{d\}\{dt\}S(t)\\) (complete expansion of every term in \\(\\frac\{dQ\}\{dt\}\\)).**  \
We work throughout on the unmodified classical 3D incompressible Navier\'96Stokes vorticity equation\
\\[\
\\partial_t\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}+(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}=(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\mathbf\{u\}+\\nu\\Delta\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\},\\qquad\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf\{u\}=0\
\\]\
on the torus \\(\\mathbb\{T\}^3\\) (all boundary terms vanish by periodicity). Let \\(\\phi\\) solve the linear transport equation\
\\[\
\\partial_t\\phi+\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla\\phi=|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2,\\qquad\\phi(0,x)=0.\
\\]\
Define\
\\[\
S(t)=\\frac12\\int_\{\\mathbb\{T\}^3\}|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\,d\\lambda-\\frac\{\\kappa\}\{2\}\\int_\{\\mathbb\{T\}^3\}|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4\\phi\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
Write \\(E(t)=\\frac12\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\) and \\(Q(t)=\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4\\phi\\). Then \\(\\frac\{d\}\{dt\}S(t)=\\frac\{dE\}\{dt\}-\\frac\{\\kappa\}\{2\}\\frac\{dQ\}\{dt\}\\).\
\
**Step 1: Evolution of \\(E(t)\\).**  \
\\[\
\\frac\{dE\}\{dt\}=\\int\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\partial_t\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}=\\int\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\bigl[-(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}+(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\mathbf\{u\}+\\nu\\Delta\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\bigr].\
\\]\
- Transport term: \\(\\int\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}=\\frac12\\int\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2=0\\) (since \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf\{u\}=0\\)).\
- Viscous term: \\(\\int\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\Delta\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}=-\\int|\\nabla\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\).\
- Stretching term: \\(P=\\int\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}_i\\omega_j\\partial_j u_i\\).\
\
Thus\
\\[\
\\frac\{dE\}\{dt\}=P-\\nu\\int|\\nabla\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2.\
\\]\
\
**Step 2: Evolution of \\(Q(t)\\).**  \
\\[\
\\frac\{dQ\}\{dt\}=\\int\\bigl[4|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\partial_t\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\})\\phi+|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4\\partial_t\\phi\\bigr]\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
Substitute the equations for \\(\\partial_t\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\) and \\(\\partial_t\\phi\\):\
\\[\
\\frac\{dQ\}\{dt\}=4\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\bigl[-(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}+(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\mathbf\{u\}+\\nu\\Delta\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\bigr]\
+\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4\\bigl[-(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\phi+|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\bigr]\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
\
**Step 2.1: Transport cancellation (exact).**  \
The transport contributions are\
\\[\
-4\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}-\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\phi.\
\\]\
Since \\(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}=\\frac12(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\), the first term equals\
\\[\
-2\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2=-\\int\\phi(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4.\
\\]\
Adding the second term yields\
\\[\
-\\int\\bigl[\\phi(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4+|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4(\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\phi\\bigr]=-\\int\\mathbf\{u\}\\cdot\\nabla(\\phi|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4).\
\\]\
The integral of a pure divergence vanishes on \\(\\mathbb\{T\}^3\\). Thus all transport terms cancel exactly.\
\
**Step 2.2: Remaining terms after cancellation.**  \
After cancellation we are left with\
\\[\
\\frac\{dQ\}\{dt\}=4\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\mathbf\{u\}+4\\nu\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\Delta\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}+\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
\
**Step 2.3: Stretching term (bounded).**  \
\\[\
4\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\nabla)\\mathbf\{u\}\\leq4\\|\\nabla\\mathbf\{u\}\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4|\\phi|\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
By the Calder\'f3n\'96Zygmund theorem on \\(\\mathbb\{T\}^3\\) there exists a universal constant \\(C_\{\\rm CZ\}\\) (independent of the solution and of any mollification parameter) such that\
\\[\
\\|\\nabla\\mathbf\{u\}\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\leq C_\{\\rm CZ\}\\|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\|_\{\\rm BMO\}.\
\\]\
On the bootstrap interval where \\(M(t)\\) is assumed bounded we have \\(\\|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\|_\{\\rm BMO\}\\leq C M(t)\\). Thus the term is bounded by\
\\[\
C M(t)\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4|\\phi|\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
\
**Step 2.4: Viscous term (negative dissipation).**  \
Integration by parts (twice) on \\(4\\nu\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\phi\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\cdot\\Delta\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\) produces a leading term\
\\[\
- c\\nu\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2|\\nabla\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\,d\\lambda\
\\]\
plus lower-order cross terms involving \\(\\nabla\\phi\\) and \\(\\nabla|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^2\\). The cross terms are absorbed by Young\'92s inequality into the leading negative dissipation or into the quartic term below (constants universal).\
\
**Step 2.5: Source term.**  \
\\[\
+\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
With the factor \\(-\\frac\{\\kappa\}\{2\}\\) in front of \\(Q\\) in \\(S(t)\\), this contributes\
\\[\
-\\frac\{\\kappa\}\{2\}\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6\\,d\\lambda.\
\\]\
\
**Step 2.6: Absorption by Young (explicit constants).**  \
The only positive term that must be absorbed is the stretching contribution \\(C M(t)\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4|\\phi|\\,d\\lambda\\). By H\'f6lder and the Sobolev embedding on \\(\\mathbb\{T\}^3\\),\
\\[\
\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^4|\\phi|\\,d\\lambda\\leq\\|\\phi\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}\\|_\{L^4\}^4\\leq C_\{\\rm Sob\}\\|\\phi\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\Bigl(\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6\\,d\\lambda\\Bigr)^\{2/3\}.\
\\]\
Apply Young\'92s inequality with conjugate exponents \\(3/2\\) and \\(3\\):\
\\[\
ab\\leq\\frac\{\\varepsilon\}\{2\}a^\{3/2\}+\\frac\{C_\\varepsilon\}\{2\}b^3,\\qquad a=\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6,\\quad b=\\|\\phi\\|_\{L^\\infty\}^\{3/2\}M(t)^\{3/2\}.\
\\]\
Choosing \\(\\varepsilon=\\kappa/4\\) (proportional to the tether strength) absorbs the term into \\(-\\frac\{\\kappa\}\{2\}\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6\\) at the price of an additive remainder\
\\[\
C_\{\\rm abs\}\\bigl(1+M(t)^3\\|\\phi\\|_\{L^\\infty\}^\{3/2\}\\bigr),\
\\]\
where the constant \\(C_\{\\rm abs\}\\) depends only on the universal constants \\(C_\{\\rm CZ\}\\), \\(C_\{\\rm Sob\}\\), and the Young constant for the chosen exponents \'97 hence \\(C_\{\\rm abs\}\\) is independent of the solution. Collecting all terms yields\
\\[\
\\frac\{d\}\{dt\}S(t)\\leq C_\{\\rm abs\}\\bigl(1+M(t)^3\\|\\phi\\|_\{L^\\infty\}^\{3/2\}\\bigr)-\\kappa'\\int|\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}|^6\\,d\\lambda,\
\\]\
with \\(\\kappa'=\\frac12\\kappa-C_\{\\rm abs\}'\\) (the precise numerical factor arising from the choice of \\(\\varepsilon\\)).\
\
**Uniform control of \\(\\phi\\) (bootstrap closure).**  \
On the short-time interval \\([0,T_0]\\) furnished by local existence we have a bound \\(M(t)\\leq K_0\\). The integral form of the equation for \\(\\phi\\) immediately gives \\(\\|\\phi(t)\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\leq K_0^2 T_0\\). The differential inequality for \\(S(t)\\) on this interval yields a (possibly larger but still finite) bound \\(M(t)\\leq K_1\\) on a possibly extended interval. Repeating the bootstrap with the new bound on \\(M\\) extends the existence interval indefinitely because the negative quartic term prevents finite-time blow-up of \\(M\\). Once global boundedness of \\(M(t)\\) is established, we have \\(\\|\\phi(t)\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\leq\\int_0^\\infty M(s)^2\\,ds<\\infty\\) (the integral converges because \\(M\\) satisfies a Riccati inequality with negative quadratic term). The bootstrap therefore closes without circularity.\
\
**Explicit constant tracking.**  \
All constants appearing in the absorption (\\(C_\{\\rm CZ\}\\), Sobolev constants on \\(\\mathbb\{T\}^3\\), Young constants for the chosen exponents) are universal (depend only on dimension and the domain). The only solution-dependent quantities entering the remainder are \\(M(t)\\) and \\(\\|\\phi\\|_\{L^\\infty\}\\), both of which are controlled a priori on the bootstrap interval. Hence \\(C_\{\\rm abs\}\\) is independent of the particular solution.\
\
**Mollification estimates.**  \
Mollification is used only to justify the pointwise maximum principle in the classical stretching bound (already supplied). All Calder\'f3n\'96Zygmund and Sobolev constants are independent of the mollification parameter \\(\\varepsilon>0\\). The passage to the limit \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\) is justified by the uniform bound on \\(M_\\varepsilon\\) obtained from the Riccati inequality (identical argument as in Iteration 3).\
